# A stop: the run ends with the stdlib's `defect`, the way a list read past the end ends it, on every backend. For a
# precondition a caller broke (zero to a negative power, a negative square root), never for a failure a program should
# handle: a stop is not in any task's raise set, so no `fork case` has to name it, and a `note unsafe` guard is not
# promised to catch it (note/term/stdlib/semantics.md, "Two kinds of failure"). Swift writes the reason to standard error and
# then stops on an empty optional: `fatalError` is not a value of the number type, and a closure typed with an arrow
# cannot sit in a text literal.

# stop the run, saying what was wrong

mark stable

bind abandon
  take reason, like text
  like number
  case node
    text <(function () \{ throw Object.assign(new Error("Invalid"), \{ host: "@term/base", form: "defect", note: "Invalid", code: "", time: Date.now(), link: \{ thing: $reason \} \}) \})()>
  case browser
    text <(function () \{ throw Object.assign(new Error("Invalid"), \{ host: "@term/base", form: "defect", note: "Invalid", code: "", time: Date.now(), link: \{ thing: $reason \} \}) \})()>
  case rust
    text <panic!("defect: \{\}", $reason)>
  case swift
    text <(\{ () in FileHandle.standardError.write(("defect: " + $reason + String(UnicodeScalar(UInt8(10)))).data(using: .utf8)!); return Optional<Int>.none! \})()>
  case kotlin
    text <throw IllegalStateException("defect: " + $reason)>
